\abstract{The Lorentz Transformation equation can be constructed from several distinct sets of assumptions.  Common approaches include requiring that (1)  points of a given velocity are rendered stationary.  (2) speed of light in opposite directions are rendered isotropic (3) speed of light is constant, (4) the coordinate system preserves scale.  (5) eigenvectors in the $x=ct$ and $x=-ct$ direction may change length but not direction.  (6) the transformation is linear (7) the Lorentz Transformation obtains a form of $M_{LT}(-v)=M_{LT}^{-1}(v)$.

This paper compares three derivations built from different subbsets of these assumptions.  The first, my preferred construction, uses (1), (2), (3), (4).  The second uses  (1) , (2), and (7).  The third follows the structure of Einstein's 1920 argument, applying (5), (6), (1) and (4).  

In the first method, the sequence$(1)\to(2)\to(3)\to(4)$, is order dependent:   Each step accomplishes a specific geometric adjustment that preserves the results of earlier steps and is preserved by later ones.  In contrast, assumption (7) determines the effects of (3) and (4) simultaneosly, though its justification appears to rely more on analogy than on geometric necessity.}

\section{The Decomposition}
\label{sec:One}
In \HREF{https://www.desmos.com/calculator/dwejxxbmpi}{https://www.desmos.com/calculator/dwejxxbmpi} (Line 16) I decomposed the actual Lorentz Transformations into four steps:

\Align{\label{One}\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&=\Pmatrix{g&0\\0&1}\Pmatrix{1&0\\0&h}\Pmatrix{1&0\\w&1}\Pmatrix{1&-v\\0&1}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Apply the Galilean (spatial skew) Transformation 
$$\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct}=\Pmatrix{1&-v/c\\0&1}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}$$
\item Apply a time skew operation in order to get an isotropic speed of light
$$\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct_1}=\Pmatrix{1&0\\w&1}\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct}$$
\item Apply time dilation and length dilation in order to normalize the determinant and restore the correct light-cone slope.
$$\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct'}=\Pmatrix{1&0\\0&h}\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct_1}$$
$$\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}=\Pmatrix{g&0\\0&1}\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct'}$$
\end{enumerate}


In the demonstration, the user is free to choose all parameters with a slider, and confirm by sight when each goal is met.  However, obtaining these goals do not achieve any final formula. 

The goal of Sections 1.1-1.4 is to find closed-form mathematical expressions that achieve each of these necessary goals. 

In the Desmos demonstration, the reciprocity of $g$ and $h$ is adopted for convenience, but section 1.4 shows that this reciprocity follows from demanding a unit determinant for $M_{LT}$.

\subsection{Apply the Galilean Skew}
\label{sec:OneDotOne}
\qbox{\textbf{Goal:}  We desire to take a trajectory $x=vt$ and skew the entire diagram so that it obtains $x_1=0$.  Once this is done, the operations of time-skew, temporal dilation, and spatial dilation will not affect the direction of this axis. \textbf{Visualization:} If the spacetime is like a stack of photographs, this operation simply takes that stack and skews them.
}

$v$ is simply the Galilean velocity.  Defining $\varphi:=\arctanh(v/c)$ the expression becomes, and defining $M_{LT}(\varphi)$ to be the associated Lorentz transformation matrix, we obtain:

\Align{\label{Two}\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}=
\describe{M_{LT}(\varphi)}{\Pmatrix{g&0\\0&1}
\Pmatrix{1&0\\0&h}
\Pmatrix{1&0\\w&1}
\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\0&1}}
\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
}

The first operation tilts the entire spacetime diagram so the world-line $x=vt$ becomes vertical.  Once this is done, later operations cannot change its direction.

\subsection{Apply a Temporal Skew}
\label{sec:OneDotTwo}
\qbox{\textbf{Problem:}  Our Galilean Skew has produced an anisotropy in the speed of light.  After the Galilean skew, the right-moving and left-moving light rays have slopes $c-v$ and $-(c+v)$, which are no longer symmetric.

We wish to re-establish the isotropy via a ``desync'' operation, or ``temporal skew''.   \textbf{Assurance:} Because the temporal skew is applied along lines of constant $x_1$, it does not change the direction of the worldlines made vertical in step 1. \textbf{Visualization} The deck of playing cards are glued together after the skew and re-sliced along lines $x_1=\text{constant}$.  The new deck is skewed along this perpendicular axis.
}

To find $w$ let's think about the speed-of-light equations:  In (a1, a2), we imagine two rays of light going in opposite directions in the x-axis.  (b1, b2, c1, c2) apply the Galilean transformations which gives us a new variable $x_1$.  (d1, d2) apply a temporal skew to the formulas and give us a new variable $t_1$

\Align{\label{Three}x\overset{a1}=ct&\qquad x\overset{a2}=-ct
\\x_1+vt\overset{b1}=ct&\qquad x_1+vt\overset{b2}=-ct
\\x_1\overset{c1}=(c-v)t&\qquad x_1\overset{c2}=-(c+v)t
\\x_1\overset{d1}=(c-v)(t_1+wx_1)&\qquad x_1\overset{d2}=-(c+v)(t_1+wx_1)
\\x_1\overset{e1}=ct_1-vt_1+wcx_1-wvx_1&\qquad x_1\overset{e2}=-vt_1-ct_1-vwx_1-cwx_1
\\x_1(1-wc+wv)\overset{f1}=(c-v)t_1&\qquad 
x_1(1+vw+cw)\overset{f2}=-t_1(c+v)
\\\frac{x_1}{t_1}\overset{g1}{=}\frac{c-v}{1+w(v-c)}&\qquad\frac{x_1}{t_1}\overset{g_2}{=}-\frac{c+v}{1+w(c+v)}
\\\left(\frac{x_1}{t_1}\right)_{\text{right}}&\overset{h1}=-\left(\frac{x_1}{t_1}\right)_{\text{left}}
\\\frac{c-v}{1+w(v-c)}&\overset{h2}{=}+\frac{c+v}{1+w(c+v)}
\\(c-v)(1+w(c+v))&\overset i=+(c+v)(1+w(v-c))
\\(c-v)+w(c^2-v^2)&\overset j=c+v+w(v^2-c^2)
\\(c-v)-(c+v)&\overset k=-2w(v^2-c^2)
\\w&\overset l=\frac{v}{v^2-c^2}=\frac{-v}{c^2-v^2}}

(a1, a2) - Light rays in the original frame.  (b1, b2) Galilean skew applied.  (c1, c2) Left and right speed-of-light lines in new coordinates.  (d1,d2) Apply the temporal skew with $w$ unknown.  (e, f) expand and collect terms.  (g1, g2) compute slopes of left and right speed of light lines.  (h) set slopes equal and opposite (isotropy condition).  (i, j, k, l) solve for w.

Combining the first two operations, 

\Align{\label{Four}\Pmatrix{1&0\\-\frac{v}{c^2-v^2}&1}\Pmatrix{1&-v\\0&1}&=\Pmatrix{1&v\\-\frac{v}{c^2-v^2}&\frac{v^2}{c^2-v^2}+1}
\\\Pmatrix{x_1\\ct_1}&\overset{c=1}=\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi
\\-\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\sinh^2\varphi+1}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\&=\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\-\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\cosh^2\varphi}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}}

This matrix is the combined effect of the Galilean skew and the temporal skew, expressed in rapidity form using $v/c=\tanh\varphi$.


\subsection{Obtain a relation $h(g)$ to make the speed of light constant in $(x',t')$-coordinates}
\label{sec:OneDotThree}
\qbox{\textbf{Successes:} We have rendered the $x=vt$ line stationary in $(x1,t)$ coordinates, and it remains stationary in $(x_1,t_1)$ coordinates.  We have obtained isotropic speed-of-light in $(x_1,t_1)$ coordinates. \textbf{Problem:} the speed of light in the $(x_1,t_1)$ coordinates is not $c$.  \textbf{Goal:} We require that the null directions remain null after the dilation steps.  That is, the images of $x=ct$ and $x=-ct$ must satisfy $x'=ct'$ and $x'=-ct'$.  This condition determines the ratio of our time-dilation factor, $h$ and our length dilation factor $g$. \textbf{Visualization:} The deck is glued back together.  $g$, the length-dilation term is left as is, and $h$ is stretched or compressed until the speed-of-light lines have slope $c$.}

Equation \eqref{One} now simplifies to 

\Align{\label{Five}
\\\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&=\Pmatrix{g&0\\0&h}\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\-\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\cosh^2\varphi}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}}

We require that $(x,ct)=(ct,ct)$ maps to $(x',ct')=(ct',ct')$ and $(x,ct)=(-ct,ct)$ maps to $(x',ct')=(-ct',ct')$.  Or in other words 

It would be interesting to see if we could solve for $g$ and $h$ independently without requiring that they are reciprocal.  To solve for $h$, we could obtain the equation

\newcommand{\toprow}[2]{g(1,-\tanh\varphi)\cdot(#1,#2)}
\newcommand{\botrow}[2]{h\cosh\varphi(-\sinh\varphi,\cosh\varphi)\cdot(#1,#2)}

\Align{\label{Six}ct'_{x=ct}&\overset{a}=\botrow{ct}{ct}
\\&=hct\cosh\varphi(\cosh\varphi-\sinh\varphi)
\\&=hct\cosh\varphi \exp(-\varphi)
\\ct'_{x=-ct}&\overset b=\botrow{-ct}{ct}
\\&=hct\cosh\varphi(\cosh\varphi+\sinh\varphi)
\\&=hct\cosh\varphi\exp(\varphi)
\\x'_{t=x/c}&\overset c=\toprow{x}{c(x/c)}
\\&=gx- gx\tanh\varphi
\\&=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}(\cosh\varphi-\sinh\varphi)
\\&=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}\exp(-\varphi)
\\x'_{t=-x/c}&\overset d=\toprow{x}{c(-x/c)}
\\&=gx+ gx\tanh\varphi
\\&=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}(\cosh\varphi+ \sinh\varphi)
\\&=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}\exp(\varphi)
}

Since $x=ct$ and $t=x/c$ are the same geometric line, their images under the transformation must agree.  The result for $x=ct$ is that $ct'=hct\cosh\varphi e^\varphi$.  The result for $t=x/c$ is that $x'=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}e^\varphi$.  We also want to impose that $x'=ct'$ when $x=ct$.  This requires

\Align{\label{Seven}ct'_{x=ct}&\overset a=x'_{t=x/c}
\\hct\cosh\varphi \exp(-\varphi)&\overset b=\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}\exp(-\varphi)
\\h\cosh\varphi&\overset c=\frac{g}{\cosh\varphi}
\\\cosh^2\varphi&\overset d=\frac{g}{h}
\\h&\overset e=g/\cosh^2\varphi}

In line (c) $x$ and $ct$ are equal, which is why they cancel.

The same result occurs when we impose that $x'=-ct'$ when $x=-ct$.  This requires

\Align{\label{Eight}ct'_{x=-ct}&\overset a=-x'_{t=-x/c}
\\
hct\cosh\varphi \exp(\varphi)&\overset b=-\frac{gx}{\cosh\varphi}\exp(\varphi)
\\h\cosh\varphi&\overset c=-\left(-\frac{g}{\cosh\varphi}\right)
\\h&\overset d=g/\cosh^2\varphi}

This reveals the ratio of our length and time dilation factor needed to restore constant $c$ after our prior steps.  This ratio provides a constancy of $c$ but we need an additional constraint to assure that space and time have not both grown by the same proportion.

\subsection{Find $g$ and $h(g)$ so the coordinate system neither shrinks or grows.}
\label{sec:OneDotFour}

\qbox{\textbf{Successes:}  We now have a matrix, such that no matter how we set $g$ the $x=vt$ line has been rendered ``stationary'', and the speed of light lines, $x=ct$ and $x=-ct$ lines remain speed-of-light lines.  
\textbf{Problem:}  Scaling $g$ or $h$ with the established relation of $h=g/\cosh^2\varphi$ results in an overall stretching or compression of the space-time.   Both the spatial and temporal dimensions stretch in proportion to $g$ so the overall transformation preserves the speed of light.  \textbf{Goal:} Find a value of $g$ and $h=g/\cosh^2\varphi$ so that the overall transformation does not stretch or compress. \textbf{Visualization} The overall transformation takes a square, squeezing or expanding it along the $x=\pm ct$ axes, making an elongated diamond.  The result of this step will make it so that the squeesing factor along the $x=ct$ axis is reciprocal of the expanding factor along the $x=-ct$ axis.  This keeps the resulting diamond the same size.}

Our Equation \eqref{One} (defining $M_{LT}(\varphi)$ to be the full Lorentz Transformation Matrix), is now

\Align{\label{Nine}\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&\overset a=g\Pmatrix{1&0\\0&\frac{1}{\cosh^2\varphi}}\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\-\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\cosh^2\varphi}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\&\overset b=g\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\-\tanh\varphi&1}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\M_{LT}(\varphi)\Pmatrix{x\\ct}:&\overset c=\Pmatrix{g&- g\tanh\varphi\\- g\tanh\varphi&g}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
}

The formula for inverse of a $2\times 2$ matrix should be familiar:

\Align{\label{Ten}\Pmatrix{g&- g\tanh\varphi\\- g\tanh\varphi&g}^{-1}&\overset a=\frac{\Pmatrix{g& g\tanh\varphi\\ g\tanh\varphi&g}}{g^2-g^2\tanh^2\varphi}
\\&\overset b=\frac{g}{g^2}\frac{\Pmatrix{1&\tanh\varphi\\ \tanh\varphi&1}}{1-\tanh^2\varphi}
\\M_{LT}(\varphi)^{-1}(\varphi)&\overset c=\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}\Pmatrix{1&\tanh\varphi\\\tanh\varphi&1}
\\&\overset d=\frac{1}{h}\Pmatrix{1&\tanh\varphi\\\tanh\varphi&1}}

We have now definitely derived these:

\Align{\label{Eleven}h&=\frac{g}{\cosh^2\varphi}
\\M_{LT}(\varphi)&=g\Pmatrix{1&-\tanh\varphi\\-\tanh\varphi&1}
\\M_{LT}(\varphi)^{-1}&=\frac{1}{h}\Pmatrix{1&\tanh(\varphi)\\\tanh(\varphi)&1}}

From these three derived facts can we make the claim that $g=\frac 1 h$?  

Yes:  To avoid artificial inflation or contraction of the coordinate grid, we impose the condition that the transformation preserve the overall scale of space-time. In matrix terms, this is equivalent to requiring the determinant, $det(M_{LT}=1$ since any uniform scaling of the coordinate grid would multiply the determinant by that scale factor.

The result of assuming that the coordinate system neither shrink nor grow is as follows:

\Align{\label{Twelve}|L|&\overset a=|L^{-1}|=1
\\1&\overset b=g^2(1-\tanh^2\varphi)
\\\cosh^2\varphi&\overset c=g^2
\\g&\overset d=\cosh\varphi
\\h&\overset e=\frac{g}{\cosh^2\varphi}=\frac{1}{\cosh\varphi}
\\M_{LT}(\varphi)\Pmatrix{x\\ct}&=\Pmatrix{\cosh\varphi&-\sinh\varphi\\-\sinh\varphi&\cosh\varphi}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
}

These values reproduce the standard Lorentz Transformation, completing the four-step geometric construction


