\section{Stretching the Eigenvectors: Einstein's 1920 Proof}
\label{sec:Two}

The following treatment recasts Einstein’s Appendix I derivation in terms of eigenvectors and scaling factors $\lambda$ and $\mu$. It is not a verbatim reproduction of his argument, but a modern algebraic reinterpretation of the same starting assumptions.  

In this section, we approach the problem in a manner more similar to what Einstein did in Appendix 1 of his 1920 book, \textit{``Relativity The Special \& the General Theory''}\cite{Einstein1920}

Einstein posited that if $x-ct=0$ (for a ray of light moving to the right) before a Lorentz Transformation then $x'-ct'=0$ after a Lorentz Transformation.  Likewise, if $x+ct=0$ (for a ray of light moving to the left) before the same Lorentz Transformation, then $x'+ct'=0$ for the same Lorentz Transformation.

Now in order to get to the next step in Einstein's proof, I am reasoning that the vector $(x_r,ct)=(ct,ct)$ ought be stretched (or contracted) to $(x_r',ct')=(\lambda ct,\lambda ct)=(\lambda x_r,\lambda ct)$ while the vector $(x_l,ct)=(-ct,ct)$ ought be stretched (or contracted) to $(x_l',ct')=(-\mu ct,\mu ct)=(-\mu x_l,\mu ct)$.

So the conditions that are met are distinct.  The condition involving $\lambda$ holds for the photon traveling to the right.  The condition involving $\mu$ holds for the photon traveling to the left.

\Align{\label{Sixteen}(x_r'-ct')&=\lambda(x_r-ct)=0
\\(x_l'+ct')&=\mu(x_l+ct))=0}

But Einstein does not stop here.  His extension is that these equations hold for all events $(x,t)$ mapping to $(x',t')$.  That is, that the entire transformation is accomplished simply by stretching the path of the right photon by a factor of $\lambda$ and stretching the path of the left photon by a factor of $\mu$

In mathematical terms, the bold claim is:

\Align{\label{Seventeen}\forall(x,t,x',t'): (x'-ct')&\overset a=\lambda(x-ct)
\\\forall(x,t,x',t'): (x'+ct')&\overset b=\mu(x+ct))}

This could be called ``global linear extension''.

\subsection{Can the ``bold claim'' be proven? (global linear extension)}
\label{sec:TwoDotOne}

Some might note that assuming the transformation is linear, the ``bold claim'' that both equations hold for all event pairs $(x,t)\to(x',t')$ is already baked in before (\eqref{Sixteen}) is written.  Because the transformation is linear, specifying its action on the two null directions determines its action on all vectors.

I prefer to be more folksy, I suppose.  I imagine that the case of constant $-c$ for the left-bound photon and the case of constant $+c$ for the right-bound photon are at first to be taken as independent facts.  

What looks like a bold claim is actually just the consequence of assuming linearity.

Still, it is worth pausing here, because ``just the consequence of assuming linearity'' may sound simple or everyday, when in fact something subtle is happening.  If we do the calculations for the right-bound photon, we have $x=ct$, The $\lambda$ equation collapses to give no information:  $$0=0$$, while the other equation $$2ct'=2\mu ct$$ scales.  

Each null direction is an eigenvector of one of the two equations, so one equation collapses to $0=0$ while the other produces a scaling relation.

The velocity of the right-bound photon is still $+c$ but it has stretched both in space and time by a factor of $\mu$.

Likewise, for the left-bound photon $x=-ct$, the $\mu$ equation collapses to $0=0$, while the $\lambda$ equation becomes a non-trivial scaling relation.

This may seem odd at first, but it is exactly what linearity requires.  Each null direction is an \textbf{eigenvector} of one of the two equations, so it satisfies that equation trivially, and the other non-trivially.  

For intervals $(x,t)$ where $x\ne \pm ct$, neither the $\lambda$ equation nor the $\mu$ equation will collapse into ``no information'', so neither equation collapses:  Both $\lambda$ and $\mu$ contribute to the expressions for $(x',t')$.  

So, my ``folksy'' description is a bit out of order.  Equation \eqref{Seventeen} already holds for both the photon going to the left, and the photon going to the right, even when Equation \eqref{Sixteen} was first introduced.

\qbox{Goal:  Find the relationship between $\mu$ and $\lambda$ by assuming that the transformation neither grows nor shrinks the coordinate system.}

If both these claims are universally true, then we can add equations (\eqref{Fifteen}a) and (\eqref{Fifteen}b) to find $x'$, and subtract (\eqref{Fifteen}a) from (\eqref{Fifteen}b) to find $ct'$ as follows:

\Align{\label{Eighteen}\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&=\Pmatrix{\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}\\\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}
}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}}

This matrix is the unique linear map that sends the two null directions to their scaled images.

We can apply the condition that the determinant of this matrix must be 1 (so that the spacetime does not shrink or grow.  This is exactly the same condition that was applied in Section 1.4.

\Align{\label{Nineteen}1&=\det\Pmatrix{\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}\\\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}}
\\&=\frac{(\mu^2+2\mu\lambda+\lambda^2)-(\mu^2-2\mu\lambda+\lambda^2)}{4}
\\&=\mu\lambda}

This means $\mu$ and $\lambda$ are reciprocals.

\qbox{Goal: Assure that $x/t=v$ is mapped to $\frac{x'}{t'}=0$}

We also wish to make it so that this transformation will map $\frac{x}{ct}=\frac{v}{c}$ to $\frac{x'}{ct'}=0$.  This is exactly the same condition that was applied in Section 1.1.

\Align{\label{Twenty}x'&=\Pmatrix{\frac{1/\lambda+\lambda}{2}&\frac{1/\lambda-\lambda}{2}}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\0&=\Pmatrix{\frac{1/\lambda+\lambda}{2}&\frac{1/\lambda-\lambda}{2}}\Pmatrix{vt\\ct}
\\0&=\frac{v}{\lambda}+v\lambda+\frac{c}{\lambda}-c\lambda
\\(c-v)\lambda&=\frac{c+v}{\lambda}
\\\lambda^2&=\frac{c+v}{c-v}}

If we allow that $\tanh\varphi=\frac{v}{c}$ this resolves as follows:

\Align{\label{TwentyOne}\lambda^2&=\frac{1+\tanh\varphi}{1-\tanh\varphi}
\\&=\frac{\cosh\varphi+\sinh\varphi}{\cosh\varphi-\sinh\varphi}
\\&=\frac{\exp{\varphi}}{\exp(-\varphi)}
\\&=(\exp\varphi)^2
\\\lambda&=\exp(\varphi)}

The exponential form reflects the additive nature of rapidity:  successive boosts add $\varphi$, so the eigenvalue must multiply as $e^\varphi$.

The full transformation then becomes

\Align{\label{TwentyTwo}\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&=\Pmatrix{\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}\\\frac{\mu-\lambda}{2}&\frac{\mu+\lambda}{2}
}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\\Pmatrix{x'\\ct'}&=\Pmatrix{\frac{\exp(-\varphi)+\exp(\varphi)}{2}&\frac{\exp(-\varphi)-\exp(\varphi)}{2}\\\frac{\exp(-\varphi)-\exp(\varphi)}{2}&\frac{\exp(-\varphi)+\exp(\varphi)}{2}
}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
\\&=\Pmatrix{\cosh\varphi&-\sinh\varphi\\-\sinh\varphi&\cosh\varphi}\Pmatrix{x\\ct}
}

This derivation provides a third method that derives the same final result given in Section 1.6.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:Four}

The three derivations presented in this paper all arrive at the same Lorentz Transformation, but they do so by emphasizing different structural features of the mapping. Einstein’s 1920 method treats the null directions as fundamental and assumes that the transformation acts by stretching these eigenvectors. The reciprocity‑based derivation assumes the symmetry condition, from which the constancy of the speed of light and the unit determinant emerge as consequences rather than assumptions. The geometric four‑step decomposition, by contrast, isolates the operational components of the transformation - spatial skew, temporal skew, dilation, and normalization - and shows how each contributes to the final form.

These approaches are not in conflict; they are different ways of organizing the same underlying geometry. Yet the differences matter. Each method foregrounds a different conceptual element: null directions, symmetry, or operational structure. In discussions of relativity, disagreements often arise not from algebra but from participants implicitly relying on different derivation frameworks. When one person thinks in terms of eigenvectors, another in terms of reciprocity, and another in terms of operational steps, they may describe the same transformation in ways that appear incompatible even though the mathematics is identical.

By placing these three derivations side by side, the present work highlights the coherence of the Lorentz Transformation across multiple conceptual foundations. The transformation is not fragile: it does not depend on any single preferred argument. Whether one begins with null directions, symmetry, or geometric operations, the same mapping emerges. Recognizing this unity may help clarify discussions in which the Lorentz Transformation is invoked, and may reduce the conceptual friction that often arises when different derivational perspectives are mixed without acknowledgment.
