\section{Appendix: Reciprocity Proof}

\qbox{A note about the forms of $g(\varphi)$ and $h(\varphi)$}

We can now plug into (\eqref{Four}) $(x_R,ct)=(ct,ct)$ and $(x_L,ct)=(-ct,ct)$, to find $(x_,ct_1)$.  What we find for the right-bound case is $$\frac{x_1}{ct_1}=\frac{1}{\cosh^2\varphi}$$.

and for the left-bound case, we find $$\frac{x_1}{ct_1}=-\frac{1}{\cosh^2\varphi}$$

Because $\cosh\varphi$ is an even function, these slopes are unchanged when $\varphi$ is replaced by $-\varphi$

The light-velocity scaling introduced by the combined skew is therefore independent of direction.

In our next steps, we will introduce a length dilation factor $h(\varphi)=h(-\varphi)$ and a time dilation factor $g(\varphi)=g(-\varphi)$ which will map these light-velocities to back to $\pm c$.  

The evenness of $g$ and $h$ follows directly from the structure of (\eqref{Four}) and does not require assuming reciprocity.

\subsection{A different line of reasoning:}
\label{sec:OneDotFive}
A different line of reasoning\cite{RG-Post-3047-10} begins by imposing the symmetry condition 

$$M_{LT}(-v) = [M_{LT}(v)]^{-1}$$

In Section 1.2 we observed that the combined Galilean and temporal skew already produces light‑cone slopes that depend only on $\cosh\varphi$, an even function. This derived symmetry is weaker than the full matrix reciprocity condition, but it provides a natural motivation for exploring whether a stronger symmetry might determine the remaining parameters directly.  The following extends from the reasoning of Section 1.2 that $g(\varphi)$ and $h(\varphi)$ are even functions.


In section 3 (Narrative Direction and Cohesion), I maintain a neutral stance on whether this is physically better reasoning, however, I feel it is definitely \textit{different} reasoning.

At first glance, one might attempt to replace step 4 of our earlier derivation with this condition.  Doing so indeed reproduces the same result.

\Align{\label{Thirteen}M_{LT}(-\varphi)&\overset a=M_{LT}^{-1}(\varphi)
\\\Pmatrix{g&-g\tanh(-\varphi)\\-g\tanh(-\varphi)&g}&\overset b=\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}\Pmatrix{1&\tanh\varphi\\\tanh\varphi&1}
\\\Pmatrix{g&g\tanh\varphi\\g\tanh\varphi&g}&\overset c=\Pmatrix{\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}&\frac{\cosh^2\varphi\tanh\varphi}{g}\\\cosh^2\varphi\tanh\varphi&\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}}
}

This yields $g=\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}$, which, when $g$ is constrained to be positive, results in $g=\cosh\varphi$.

However, the inverse condition is actually more powerful than this limited application suggests.

\qbox{Question:  Could this condition have been used to simultaneously solve for $h$ and $g$ from (\eqref{Five})?}

Yes.

\Align{\label{Fourteen}M_{LT}(\varphi)&\overset a=\Pmatrix{g&-g\tanh\varphi\\-h\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&h\cosh^2\varphi}
\\
M_{LT}^{-1}(\varphi)&\overset b=\frac{\Pmatrix{h\cosh^2\varphi&g\tanh\varphi\\h\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&g}}{gh(\cosh^2\varphi-\sinh^2\varphi)}
\\&\overset c=\Pmatrix{\boxed{\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}}&\frac{\tanh\varphi}{h}\\\frac{\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi}{g}&\boxed{\frac{1}{h}}}
\\M_{LT}(-\varphi)&\overset d=\Pmatrix{\boxed{g}&g\tanh\varphi\\h\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\boxed{h\cosh^2\varphi}}
\\\describe{M_{LT}^{-1}(\varphi)}{\Pmatrix{\boxed{\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g}}&\frac{\tanh\varphi}{h}\\\frac{\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi}{g}&\boxed{\frac{1}{h}}}}&\overset e=\describe{M_{LT}(-\varphi)}{\Pmatrix{\boxed{g}&g\tanh\varphi\\h\cosh\varphi\sinh\varphi&\boxed{h\cosh^2\varphi}}}
\\\boxed{g=\frac{\cosh^2\varphi}{g} }&\to \boxed{g=\cosh\varphi}
\\\boxed{\frac 1 h=h\cosh^2\varphi} &\to \boxed{h=\frac{1}{\cosh\varphi}}
}

Hence, imposing the symmetry condition is sufficient to start from (\eqref{Five}) and give us both $g=\cosh\varphi$ and $h=\frac{1}{\cosh\varphi}$.  Thus when the reciprocity condition is assumed, the constancy of the speed of light, and the unitary deteriminant emerge as results, instead of as independent assumptions.

Section \ref{sec:Three} discusses how this reciprocity‑based derivation fits into the overall narrative and why it constitutes a distinct proof.


\subsection{Narrative Direction and Cohesion}
\label{sec:Three}

We have actually now covered not two, but three proofs of the Lorentz Transformation in this paper.

The first and second derivations both begin by (Section 1.1) establishing that a world-line $x=x_0+vt$ is mapped to a stationary point $x=x_0$, and (Section 1.2) a temporal skew that forces the speed of light to be the same in both directions.  

My original derivation then states (Section 1.3) that the speed of light ought be $c$ after the transformation and (Section 1.4) that the overall  coordinate system must neither shrink or grow.  From these, we derive the Lorentz transformation and from these reciprocity follows as a result.

The second derivation goes a different direction, assuming reciprocity (section 1.5).  But I did not give a narrative rationale for making the mathematical assumption.  We could make the assumption via analogy.  With regards to the Galilean transformation and rotation transformation, we can see

\Align{\label{TwentyThree}M_{\text{Galilean}}(v)&\overset a=\Pmatrix{1&-v\\0&1}
\\M{-1}_{\text{Galilean}}(v)&\overset b=\Pmatrix{1&v\\0&1}=M_{\text{Galilean}}(-v)
\\M_{\text{Rotation}}(\theta)&\overset c=\Pmatrix{\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&\cos\theta}
\\M{\text{Rotation}}(\theta)&\overset d=\Pmatrix{\cos\theta&\sin\theta\\-\sin\theta&\cos\theta}=M_{\text{Rotation}}(-\theta)
}

Since the reciprocity relation holds for Galilean and rotation transformations, one might reasonably expect it to hold for Lorentz transformation as well.   These analogies do not prove that reciprocity must hold, but they are a plausible narrative rationale.  If reciprocity is assumed, then the constancy of the speed of light (Section 1.3) and the unitary determinant (1.4) become results of our derivation rather than assumptions.


